"CONSUMER CONTRACTS\nMut. Ins. Co., 993 N.E.2d 684, 689 (Mass. 2013) (standard insurance policies must be interpreted\nin light of \"what an objectively reasonable insured . . . would expect to be covered\") (quoting\nGolchin v. Liberty Mut. Ins. Co. (Golchin 1), 950 N.E.2d 853, 857 (Mass. 2011)); Katze v.\nRandolph & Scott Mut. Fire Ins. Co., 341 N.W.2d 689, 692 (Wis. 1984) (resolving the ambiguity\nof a term by adopting the meaning that \"a reasonable person in the position of an insured would\nhave understood the words to mean\").\nEven when the meaning of a term is not uncertain or has otherwise been ascertained, courts\nmay refuse to enforce it when it conflicts with the reasonable expectations of consumers. See\nRestatement of the Law Second, Contracts § 211(3) (AM. L. INST. 1981). See, e.g., Berkson v.\nGogo LLC, 97 F. Supp. 3d 359, 391 (E.D.N.Y. 2015) (noting that the formation process must be\nconsistent with the reasonable expectations of the consumer, stating that \"[i]t is recognized that\nwhere the offering party has reason to believe 'that the party manifesting assent' to a contract\n'would not do so' if she 'knew that the writing contained a particular term, the term is not part of\nthe agreement\"') (quoting Restatement of the Law Second, Contracts § 211(3) (AM. L. INST.\n1981)); Sellers v. JustAnswer LLC, 289 Cal. Rptr. 3d 1, 14 (Cal. Ct. App. 2021) (stating that\n\"[c]ourts have characterized [shrinkwrap] licenses as contracts of adhesion ... but have generally\nfound them to be enforceable if consistent with the reasonable expectations of the consumer\");\nWoodruff v. Bretz, Inc., 218 P.3d 486 (Mont. 2009) (holding that an arbitration provision in a\nstandard-form purchase contract for a motor home was not enforceable against the purchaser\nbecause it was not within the purchaser's reasonable expectations); Lauvetz v. Alaska Sales & \nServ., 828 P.2d 162 (Alaska 1991) (the reasonable-expectations doctrine applied to set aside\nexclusions to a collision-damage waiver (CDW)); C & J Fertilizer, Inc. v. Allied Mut. Ins. Co.,\n227 N.W.2d 169 (Iowa 1975) (the reasonable-expectations doctrine applied to override explicit\ncoverage-limiting language in an insurance policy); Rodman v. State Farm Mut. Ins. Co., 208\nN.W.2d 903, 906 (Iowa 1973) (approvingly quoting ROBERT E. KEETON, BASIC TEXT ON\nINSURANCE LAW § 6.3(a), at 751 (1971), noting that \"[t]he objectively reasonable expectations of\napplicants and intended beneficiaries regarding the terms of insurance contracts will be honored\neven though painstaking study of the policy provisions would have negated those expectations\").\n94\n@§4\n"